WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Reverse engineering is a real topic.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.210
We now have an army of tireless workers

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.440
who know a ton about everything,

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.260
low level and high level,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.750
and you can point them at how other programs work.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.810
You could try to filter that,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.160
the way the cyber filters limited security work.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.260
But there are open models, and ways around the filters.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.910
So let's assume this is here to stay.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.770
Now think about the software that

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.290
made a lot of money in the past.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.350
VisiCalc, then Lotus 1-2-3, then Excel,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.870
then Google Sheets.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.640
Windows copying Apple,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.400
Apple copying what it saw at Xerox.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.840
One SQL database after another.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.840
Was reverse engineering needed to copy those ideas?

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:48.170
No.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.270
Software is not patentable.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.870
That is a problem for doing business,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.390
but an enormous advantage for the whole discipline,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.610
which innovates at a speed more

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.510
regulated fields never see.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.200
Wherever there is money,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.870
someone gets organised and copies you.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.510
Language models just democratise that.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.910
So the point is not the reverse engineering.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.090
The point is reimplementation.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.080
A graphic designer who has used Illustrator for years,

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.520
sitting next to a programmer, a company of two,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.020
can now reimagine that product and

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.360
build it from scratch with LLMs.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.690
What is inside this software is not a trade secret.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.200
The hidden algorithms that nobody knows are by now zero.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.710
We are not in '85.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.020
And there is the clean room.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.370
You have an LLM turn code into a specification.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.610
You delete the logs, start a fresh instance,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.420
and reimplement from the specification.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.390
Often the documentation alone is enough.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.820
You could reimplement Redis under

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.960
a different licence that way.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.920
So I think this topic has been blown out of proportion.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.640
It is incredibly cool that these models

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.010
are so good at reverse engineering,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.610
and there are more positive uses than negative ones.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.300
When you reimplement,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.510
you keep the conventions a user already knows,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.550
so they don't feel like a fish out of water,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.650
and then you change a lot and make it better.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.350
The big companies have lost the ability

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.230
to turn out great products.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.110
Once, every new version was a celebration.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.190
Small teams can do that again.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.080
But flip the argument.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.280
If I can easily reproduce any software I know how to use,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.040
is that the end of software companies?

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.350
The problems there are serious.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.730
Fewer companies will make money,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.850
so fewer can hire developers.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.530
Still, I believe on the whole it ends up positive.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.700
Everyone will hold very powerful tools,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.110
and can bend them into exactly what they want.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.020
What protection is left?

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.780
The law is similar in America and Europe.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:00.700
Take AutoCAD.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.180
Nobody can stop you making a command where you pick

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.280
two points and the circle passes through them.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.430
You can copy the function, and how it operates.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.450
Only if the interface becomes too similar, the icons,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.850
the layout, can you have problems.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.920
And then someone has to sue, with a budget.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.980
That works for big companies.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.500
For small software it barely works.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.860
The precedents are not reassuring either.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:29.480
Lotus versus Borland.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.240
Apple versus Microsoft.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.230
The spreadsheet was a stroke of genius,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.880
a very specific idea,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.880
and you would expect it to be protected.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:39.820
It was not.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.250
It was replicated endless times.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.940
So where is the real protection?

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.790
In things that are truly specialised.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.370
Copying DaVinci Studio is no walk in the park.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.390
You get to 70%, and that is fine for almost everyone,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.780
but the Hollywood studio still buys DaVinci.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.060
And in the business-to-business relationship,

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.390
because nobody wants to depend on

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.350
something thrown onto GitHub.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.460
Another thing will happen.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.030
As some programmers lose their jobs,

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.000
it may go like the nineties,

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.920
when we had fewer opportunities and spent

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.400
our spare time writing open source.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.020
When programmers were in huge demand,

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.270
open source fell off tragically.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.640
Maybe now people will say, I've lost my job,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.740
I'll start a little company on free software,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.070
because keeping it closed doesn't

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.860
make much sense any more.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.860
For sure a lot of commercial value will be destroyed.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.080
But we have been through this.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.620
Linux, Redis and PHP destroyed a ton of companies.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.980
Sun was sunk by open source too.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.880
And we came out with an even more flourishing industry.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.940
It is not a given that it happens again,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.200
because this time the detonation may be bigger.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.700
But for those who do truly excellent

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.220
things there is room.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.720
AutoCAD and Photoshop were protected by excellence.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.130
If the big software houses go

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.280
back to thrilling their users,

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.090
with radical new ideas every six months

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.340
that others first have to copy,

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:14.600
that still works.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.600
And know your customer.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.840
Work with that group in mind.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.470
Ask a reasonable sum,

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:23.320
not 2000 euros for a two-year licence.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.110
Give updates, listen to the feedback,

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.880
and distil those confused ideas into

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:31.160
features that are nice to use.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:32.470
One last risk.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:35.360
Now that implementation costs so little,

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.750
the laws on software may tighten,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.430
with more patents and more copyright,

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.420
demanded loudly by lobbies.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.100
I would see that as a step backwards.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.340
Our sector is hard to work in,

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.020
but it became enormous because it is the way it is.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.730
We'll see.

